# Net renames applied by scripts/fix-suds.soap4.py to the icmem pages.
# Format: <page or *> <old name> => <new name>. Names as in the suds files.
# Every entry is a deviation from the drawing text and says why.

# iram01 re-buffers the processor PC it receives as -PCB<n> into a local
# PC<n>B; the processor board (lpc) has its own PC<n>B net with the same
# name. The generated top level wires same-named signals together, so the
# icmem copy is named after the pin it is derived from.
iram01 pc0b => pcb0
iram01 pc1b => pcb1
iram01 pc2b => pcb2
iram01 pc3b => pcb3
iram01 pc4b => pcb4
iram01 pc5b => pcb5
iram01 pc6b => pcb6
iram01 pc7b => pcb7
iram01 pc8b => pcb8
iram01 pc9b => pcb9
iram01 pc10b => pcb10
iram01 pc11b => pcb11

# olord2 makes the icmem board's own -RESET (read by olord1); the processor
# board makes its -RESET on clockd from the same RESET. Two boards, two
# nets with one name, so the icmem one gets a board prefix.
#
# MIT's pin-by-pin wire lists settle it, one file per board, each -RESET run
# with exactly one driver and no connector pin:
#   doc/ai/cadrwd/icmem3.wlr:8091  -RESET, 4 pins: 1A06-08 olord2 (74S37,
#     TO) drives 1A08-01, 1A09-01 and 1A04-01 on olord1.
#   doc/ai/cadrwd/cadr4.wlr:15038  -RESET, 9 pins: 1B18-06 clockd (74S37,
#     TO) drives 4C11-01, 3B26-01, 3D26-01, 3E12-01, 3E08-19, 1D28-12,
#     1E20-01 and 1C23-01 on the cadr pages.
# What crosses the boards is the positive RESET: icmem3.wlr:8087 has 1C08-08
# (olord2, 74S10) driving 1A06-10, that same 74S37's input, while
# cadr4.wlr:15034 has RESET as a ONE PIN RUN / NO DRIVE on 1B18-04, the
# clockd 74S37's input, so the cadr board receives it from off board. Both
# -RESET drivers therefore always carry the same value, which is why reading
# the two boards as one file and merging by name never showed a conflict.
# (uhdl's CADR4.netlist merges them; that is a reading error, not a wire-OR.)
* \-reset\ => \-icmem reset\
